This suite verifies that mgr/volumes' auto-upgrade of pre-v3 (v1 and v2)
subvolumes to the v3 layout is non-disruptive across a Ceph upgrade.

Starting from an N-1|N-2 release, the setup step creates, with all the
subvolumes below (except clones and purges) being either v1 or v2 (the
3-setup facet):

  - 3 subvolumes, each mounted by a dedicated client (a mix of kernel and
    ceph-fuse) using subvolume-scoped credentials, with a background writer
    continuously doing IO in it. Two of them get snapshots (one and two)
    taken while their clients are writing.
  - 5 async clones that are held pending until the old ceph-mgr goes away,
    by having it pick them up with a long mgr/volumes/snapshot_clone_delay
    (reset afterwards, for the new ceph-mgr). pause_cloning cancels clones,
    queued or in-progress (see https://tracker.ceph.com/issues/81383). The
    clones themselves are v2, as the old ceph-mgr creates them so. They are
    of:
      - (3 of them) a snapshot of a separate source subvolume, which has two
        snapshots.
      - the snapshot of an IO subvolume, whose client keeps writing to it.
      - the snapshot of a (v2) subvolume that is then removed with
        `fs subvolume rm --retain-snapshots`, its data dir caught mid-purge
        (as below).
  - 3 subvolume purges (`fs subvolume rm`) of v2 subvolumes, and 1 of the
    data dir of a v2 subvolume with a snapshot removed with
    `--retain-snapshots`, that are caught mid-purge and paused
    (mgr/volumes/pause_purging). The old ceph-mgr upgrades a v1 subvolume
    to v2 before removing it, and v1 subvolumes don't support retaining
    snapshots, so these (and the clone source removed with retained
    snapshots) are v2 regardless.

The v1/v2 subvolumes (other than clones and purges) are created with
non-default attributes: size (quota), uid/gid, mode, an isolated pool
namespace, an earmark and user metadata, plus a user xattr, an ACL and a
file count quota set on their data dir. Their v1/v2 snapshots get user
metadata. These must be as the old ceph-mgr reported them (`fs subvolume
info`, `fs subvolume metadata ls`, `fs subvolume snapshot info`, `fs
subvolume snapshot metadata ls`) with only ceph-mgr upgraded and after
auto-upgrade to v3. A snapshot must report pending clones while the
delayed clones of it are pending, and none once they finish. After
auto-upgrade, the subvolume boundary (where the subvolume is marked as
such, probed with hard links) must be where it is for a subvolume created
as v3.

Each subvolume (except a v1 one without snapshots) also gets an auth ID
authorized through the old ceph-mgr, with which it is mounted (at its v1/v2
data dir) by an extra ceph-fuse session. That session must survive the
upgrade and auto-upgrade. After auto-upgrade, `fs subvolume authorized_list`
must list the auth IDs authorized before (and after) it; `fs subvolume
deauthorize` must revoke the auth ID's access to both the v1/v2 and v3
paths (so that the old key can't mount the subvolume); and `fs subvolume
evict` must evict the session, mounted at the v1/v2 data dir.

v2 subvolumes are created through `fs subvolume create`, and their
credentials through `fs subvolume authorize`. No release that can be
upgraded from creates v1 subvolumes, so they are fabricated in the layout
ceph-mgr used to create them in (data and v1 snapshots in
<subvolume>/<uuid>, .meta with version 1), with the attributes set the way
ceph-mgr would, and with credentials for the data dir. The first snapshot
of a v1 subvolume is taken directly in its data dir, the rest through the
old ceph-mgr, which must handle it as v1. A v1 subvolume without snapshots
is not accessed through the old ceph-mgr, which would upgrade it to v2.

The cluster is then upgraded, either:

  - mgr-only first: the new ceph-mgr can't auto-upgrade subvolumes as the old
    MDS does not know the `ceph.dir.subvolume.prevpath` vxattr. The v1/v2
    subvolumes must keep working as such (except for a v1 subvolume without
    snapshots possibly getting upgraded to v2, as ceph-mgr always did):
    client IO and subvolume commands (getpath, info, snapshot
    create/ls/info, clone status) must succeed, and the clones and purges
    must finish. The remaining daemons are upgraded afterwards.
  - full: all daemons in one go.

After the upgrade, the first subvolume command on each subvolume must upgrade
it to v3 in place, keeping its uuid whether or not it has snapshots, while
its client keeps doing IO uninterrupted and without having to remount. The
clones and purges started before the upgrade must finish successfully (for
v1, while the clone source, and the snapshot being cloned with it, gets
upgraded). Snapshot listing must include the snapshots taken before (v1/v2)
and after (v3) the auto-upgrade, and new clones of v1/v2 snapshots must work
(both with only ceph-mgr upgraded and after the full upgrade), with data
matching the snapshot. The subvolumes removed with retained snapshots must
stay so (listed, with their snapshots), with only ceph-mgr upgraded as well
as after auto-upgrade; their retained snapshots must still be clonable after
auto-upgrade, and removing them (the last snapshot) must remove the
subvolumes.

Finally, each client is remounted at its subvolume's new v3 path twice: with
the credentials it had been using (for the v1/v2 data dir), and with new ones
from `fs subvolume authorize` after the upgrade (which must be for the v3
path). Each time, all the data written by it must be accessible and intact,
and the subvolume must be writable.

This suite should make use of N-1|N-2 releases.
